---
title: Clean Water for Every Community
order: 3
icon: droplet
---

In the 21st century, it is an outrage that hundreds of thousands of Nairobians — especially in informal settlements like Kibera, Mathare, Mukuru, and Korogocho — still lack access to clean, affordable water. Residents buy water from cartels at exorbitant prices, risking cholera and typhoid with every sip from unprotected sources. Meanwhile, Nairobi's water infrastructure leaks an estimated 40 percent of its supply before it ever reaches a tap. This is a failure of leadership, not of resources.

We demand an accelerated program of borehole drilling and rehabilitation in every ward where piped water is unreliable. Each borehole must be equipped with a solar-powered pump, a storage tank, and a community-managed water kiosk selling water at a regulated, affordable price. We will overhaul the Nairobi City Water and Sewerage Company with a clear mandate: reduce non-revenue water to below 20 percent within three years through pipe replacement, pressure management, and a modern metering system.

For communities far from the piped network, we demand water trucking contracts that are transparently tendered and publicly monitored, with GPS tracking on every truck to verify delivery. Every informal settlement must have at least one public water point per 200 households, built to Kenya Bureau of Standards specifications and managed by a elected community water committee. We will also invest in rooftop rainwater harvesting systems for all public schools and health centers across the county.

The era of opaque water project contracts is over. Every water project — its budget, contractor, timeline, and completion status — will be published on a public Water Accountability Portal. Communities will have a direct line to report broken boreholes, leaking pipes, and overcharging vendors. Clean water is not a privilege for those who can afford private connections — it is a fundamental right that the county government exists to guarantee.
